WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
REMEMBER BOOSTED?:
Remember Boosted Boards? They were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:07.520
premium electric skateboard company that was quite&nbsp;
popular back in the early 2010s largely thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:13.760
to one influencer: Casey Neistat. Casey was an&nbsp;
avid user of Boosted Boards and used them to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.520
all around New York City. And given that he was&nbsp;
vlogging on a daily basis during this time period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.000
and getting millions of views on each and every&nbsp;
video, the amount of brand exposure he got for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:29.280
Boosted was insane. All of sudden, Boosted was&nbsp;
the go to electric skateboard manufacturer for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:35.880
millennials despite often costing over $1000 per&nbsp;
board. Pretty soon, Boosted started receiving even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:41.480
more coverage from Marques Brownlee, a few viral&nbsp;
videos, and even traditional news outlets. For,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:46.720
the longest time, it seemed that Boosted was&nbsp;
truly a humble grassroots success story that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:53.280
fulfilled a specific niche, but things didn’t&nbsp;
stay that way forever. Moving into the late 2010s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.680
we started seeing viral stories pop up that&nbsp;
Boosted Boards were overheating and catching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:02.520
fire. Boosted didn’t just try to sweep this&nbsp;
under the rug either. They publicly admitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.400
the issue and announced a recall of second&nbsp;
generation boards during the holiday season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:12.680
of 2016, making it clear that they prioritized&nbsp;
consumer safety over maximizing profits. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.840
despite their goodwill, they slowly faded into the&nbsp;
background as they resolved their issues and came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.000
out with a new generation of boards. Just like&nbsp;
before, these boards would also be covered by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:28.240
Casey and a slew of other popular creators,&nbsp;
but despite the social media coverage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:33.440
sales didn’t get all that much better for&nbsp;
Boosted. They tried to diversify their business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.000
but all of their efforts to expand outside&nbsp;
of just skateboards also fell flat. And as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.880
money burning VC backed startup, the only way&nbsp;
that Boosted was gonna survive was by showing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.000
growth. And given that they didn’t have much&nbsp;
growth to show, investors would eventually pull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.760
the plug on Boosted and Boosted would file&nbsp;
for bankruptcy in mid 2020. Looking back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:02.280
it seems that just one public misstep destroyed&nbsp;
the entire company, but really, the boards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:08.320
catching fire were just the tip of the iceberg.&nbsp;
So, here’s what really happened to Boosted.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:17.960
OPTIMISTIC BEGINNINGS:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:23.840
the story of Boosted dates back to 2011 to a&nbsp;
couple of Stanford students named Sanjay Dastoor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.320
John Ullmen, and Matthew Tran. Like most&nbsp;
college students and urban dwellers in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:35.040
a daily pain point for these guys was walking&nbsp;
around campus and nearby areas. Technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:41.040
everything was super close within just a few&nbsp;
mile radius, but when you’re walking back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.920
forth between your apartment, classes, grocery&nbsp;
stores, and libraries, commuting time starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:52.640
to add up pretty quickly. The go to solution for&nbsp;
most people is to simply ride a bike, but our trio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.720
wanted a smaller simpler solution that could be&nbsp;
easily carried around without compromising on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:04.040
speed. Thus, the idea of an electric skateboard&nbsp;
was born. Our trio was by no means interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.800
slapping a motor onto a skateboard and calling&nbsp;
it a day. They wanted to create an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.280
refined and reliable experience. So, they started&nbsp;
desigining a motorized skateboard from scratch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.920
constantly tweaking and improving each aspect&nbsp;
whether that be acceleration, breaking, handling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:24.560
or durability. Fortunately, they had plenty of&nbsp;
time and resources to do this as they had secured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.880
backing from Stanford’s own startup accelerator:&nbsp;
StartX. Eventually, they would come up with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:35.880
extremely premium board that people would call&nbsp;
the “Apple of E-Boards”. And to guage just how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.280
interested people would be about a Boosted Board,&nbsp;
the trio would launch a Kickstarter campaign with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:45.880
a goal of raising $100,000. To their surprise,&nbsp;
they would hit the goal within just 1 day and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:51.280
they would end up raising nearly half a million&nbsp;
dollars. By all accounts, this was a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:57.560
success, but reading between the lines, you could&nbsp;
already see a few red flags, primarily the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:05.880
of backers. Despite raising $467,000, Boosted only&nbsp;
had a total of 1,100 backers. The average backer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:12.960
was putting up $420 meaning that to reach their&nbsp;
$100,000 goal, they only had to get 200 to 250&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:18.400
backers. Not a bad goal by any means but really&nbsp;
brings into question just how big the target&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.160
market really was. Nonetheless, Boosted would&nbsp;
continue with their marketing and go to market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:28.560
effort. These guys were young guys who understood&nbsp;
the power of social media, and as such, much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.440
their efforts went into growing their Redit and&nbsp;
Facebook pages. Sanjay even hosted a Ted Talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:38.280
that would go viral pulling in nearly 2 million&nbsp;
views. All of this traction helped Boosted raise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:43.760
another $10 million in funding but what happened&nbsp;
next was something that none of them could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.520
ever anticipated. Boosted had caught the attention&nbsp;
of up and coming YouTube star Casey Neistat who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:55.840
would single handedly boost interest in Boosted&nbsp;
by a whopping 300%. This trend would simply go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.480
into overdrive when a creator named Jesse posted&nbsp;
a video viral video of him riding a boosted board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.440
around New York City that was made to look like&nbsp;
the magic carpet from Aladdin. This video would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.600
go on to reach 35 million views. Jesse would make&nbsp;
a follow up to this video in San Francisco that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:17.880
went on to get 13 million views. And that was just&nbsp;
on Jesse’s channel itself. It’s very likely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.000
with all the reposts and media coverage, this&nbsp;
little stunt drew the attention of over 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:29.080
million people. Boosted could not have dreamed&nbsp;
of a more successful brand awareness campaign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:42.180
but Boosted would soon learn that brand awareness&nbsp;
and sales were 2 completely different things.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.480
HURDLE AFTER HURDLE:
None of this is to say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:49.040
Boosted didn’t have a roaring start. Just due to&nbsp;
the raw magnitude of exposure that they enjoyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:54.200
they obviously sold a bunch of boards,&nbsp;
though we don’t know the exact number. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.200 --> 00:06:00.400
once all of the hype settled down, Boosted was&nbsp;
left with a much trickier situation. The reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.960
was that the original problem that they were&nbsp;
trying to solve did not even benefit from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:10.880
invention. Here’s the thing, commuting on college&nbsp;
campuses is surely a hurdle for college students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:17.360
but you know what’s an even bigger hurdle?&nbsp;
Spending $400 to $500 or even over a $1000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:23.120
on a skateboard. So, aside from a few rich kids,&nbsp;
Boosted had largely boxed themselves out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:29.480
college market simply due to price. And this is&nbsp;
really a shame given that most college students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:34.560
probably knew about boosted thanks to social media&nbsp;
and many of them probably even wanted one, but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:40.640
price was simply too big to swallow. Fortunately,&nbsp;
Boosted also appealed to urban dwellers as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:46.360
but the same issue largely applied. Those who&nbsp;
walked everywhere usually couldn’t afford a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:51.480
premium electric skateboard especially with the&nbsp;
rents and general cost of living in these cities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:56.760
And those who could afford it weren’t exactly&nbsp;
the kind to be riding these around the city. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:00.880
mean could you imagine an investment banker or&nbsp;
corporate lawyer riding one of these to work on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.680
a regular basis. So really, Boosted Boards only&nbsp;
appealed to exception cases like Casey: high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:12.040
income earners who regularly traversed the city.&nbsp;
This isn’t to say YouTubers like Casey and Marques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:17.440
were capping and that Boosted Boards weren’t great&nbsp;
products. They were phenomenal products, but just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:22.600
because you have a phenomenal product doesn't mean&nbsp;
that it’ll sell. It’s the same issue that Lucid is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:28.840
facing right now. By all accounts, they’ve made a&nbsp;
car that tops not just Tesla but the EV industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:34.880
in general in virtually every category. A lot of&nbsp;
people would love to have this car, but with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:41.120
being said, there’s just not all that many people&nbsp;
who can buy a $100,000 luxury electric sedan. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:46.080
was the same thing with Boosted, and things only&nbsp;
got worse as competitors started catching wind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.040
this trend, particularly Lime. If you’re not&nbsp;
familiar with Lime Scooter, they’re the guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:55.760
who started the whole electric scooter trend.&nbsp;
Basically, they were looking to do the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.760 --> 00:08:01.680
same thing as Boosted but with 2 key differences.&nbsp;
Instead of skateboards, it was scooters which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.720
a lot easier to ride for most people. And two,&nbsp;
instead of having consumers buy their scooters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:12.880
they had people rent their scooters. Generally, a&nbsp;
Lime scooter cost a $1 to a $1.50 to start riding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:17.720
and then $0.15 to $0.39 per minute of riding&nbsp;
depending on the city. From the business side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:24.240
things, this was a terrible idea because achieving&nbsp;
profitability with this model was a nightmare. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:29.280
fact, a major competitor to Lime called Bird&nbsp;
just filed for bankruptcy last month after not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:35.520
being able to make this model work. But, from the&nbsp;
consumer side, this model was great. They could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.640
pay just a few bucks to get around the city on&nbsp;
the few occasions that they were in a rush or were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:45.360
just feeling lazy which was a lot more affordable&nbsp;
than getting a Boosted Board. Now, you could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:49.680
argue that these small transactions eventually&nbsp;
add up to being even more than the price of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:56.320
Boosted Board and you’re probably right. But, for&nbsp;
most consumers, it’s much easier to afford a $5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.800
micro charge every time they ride over a $500&nbsp;
upfront cost. And just as Boosted was struggling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:16.920
to find their core market and Lime entered the&nbsp;
scene, Boosted boards would start catching fire.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.240
THE FIERY FALL:
The who fiasco with Boosted Boards catching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:25.440
fire takes us back to late 2016 to a web developer&nbsp;
named Rick Bross, or actually more specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.600
his roomate. One day, when Rick was out, his&nbsp;
Boosted Board would start overheating and fill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:36.120
his apartment with a bunch of toxic smoke which&nbsp;
naturally frightened his roommate. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:41.160
vacating the apartment as soon as possible though,&nbsp;
the roommate would decide to record the incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:46.080
which as you would guess would end up going viral&nbsp;
on Reddit. Given that Boosted would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.920
go bankrupt, it wouldn’t have been surprising&nbsp;
if Boosted had just pushed this under the rug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:57.080
acted like it never happened, but Boosted did&nbsp;
the exact opposite, they did the right thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.080 --> 00:10:01.240
They reached out to Rick immediately, offered&nbsp;
a replacement, and wanted to take a look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:07.400
defective board to fix the issue. Boosted’s CEO&nbsp;
Sanjay took it even one step further. He would fly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:12.880
from California to New York just to meet up with&nbsp;
Rick in person and address the issue. Before they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:17.560
could resolve the issue though, another report&nbsp;
about a defective Boosted Board would come out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:23.720
and this would pretty much force Boosted to issue&nbsp;
a full recall. It turned out that the issue was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:28.280
that water was able to get into the battery&nbsp;
pack when riding which eventually caused a short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:34.280
circuit. Boosted would of course fix the issue&nbsp;
and come out with a refined version but things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:39.680
were never the same. As such, many publications&nbsp;
often point to the fire incident as being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:45.360
definitive end of Boosted but I’d argue that they&nbsp;
were already headed in that direction and that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:51.120
fire incident simply accelerated that trend. You&nbsp;
see, for Sanjay, electric skateboards was always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:57.280
just an entry point into the electric market. His&nbsp;
end goal was always to build out a whole slew of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:02.840
electric products like bikes and scooters, but&nbsp;
to his disappointment, these products never took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.840 --> 00:11:07.840
off. Sanjay was even willing to step to the side&nbsp;
and let another person have a shot at being CEO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:13.640
and expanding Boosted, but this wasn’t all that&nbsp;
successful either. Well, actually technically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.640 --> 00:11:17.880
they did continue to run decently successful&nbsp;
marketing campaigns and even partnered with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:23.320
skateboarding legend, Tony Hawke, and even&nbsp;
almost Kanye West. But see, brand awareness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:28.960
and marketing was never where Boosted fell short&nbsp;
anyway. They always crushed it from those aspects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:34.280
Where they truly fell short was product market&nbsp;
fit and profitability which remained a constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:41.160
throughout their run. And eventually in mid 2020,&nbsp;
they simply ran out of funding and had no choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.240
but to file for bankruptcy. They would sell off&nbsp;
the remaining parts of the busines to Lime and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:51.240
company who wanted to take on the Boosted&nbsp;
brand name. If you look up Boosted today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:56.880
these new owners are who show up, but these guys&nbsp;
have nothing to do with the original Boosted other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:02.200
than the name. And that’s what happened to&nbsp;
Boosted Boards. Another product that blew up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:07.160
in the 2010s only to fade into oblivion is&nbsp;
GoPro. Check out this video to learn more.

